Array Leaders

You are given an array arr of positive integers. Your task is to find all the leaders in the array.
An element is considered a leader if it is greater than or equal to all elements to its right. The rightmost element is always a leader.

Examples:

Input: arr = [16, 17, 4, 3, 5, 2]
Output: [17, 5, 2]
Explanation: Note that there is nothing greater on the right side of 17, 5 and, 2.

Input: arr = [10, 4, 2, 4, 1]
Output: [10, 4, 4, 1]
Explanation: Note that both of the 4s are in output, as to be a leader an equal element is also allowed on the right. side

Input: arr = [5, 10, 20, 40]
Output: [40]
Explanation: When an array is sorted in increasing order, only the rightmost element is leader.

Input: arr = [30, 10, 10, 5]
Output: [30, 10, 10, 5]
Explanation: When an array is sorted in non-increasing order, all elements are leaders.

Constraints:
1 <= arr.size() <= 10^6
0 <= arr[i] <= 10^6

APPROACH 1: BRUTE FORCE : using nested loop and checking for every element whether this element is greater then all or not if it is greater
                          then storing it in resultant array.

APPROACH 2: OPTIMAL APPROACH : start traversing from right side and take a variable which will store the maximum number, and while traversing if the
                              number is greater then maxi then  add that number to resultant array and update the maxi also.

CODE : 

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> leaders(vector<int>& arr) {
        int n = arr.size();
        int maxi = 0;

        vector<int> res;
        res.push_back(arr[n - 1]);
        maxi = arr[n - 1];

        n = n - 2;

        while (n >= 0) {
            if (arr[n] >= maxi) {
                res.push_back(arr[n]);
                maxi = arr[n];
            }
            n--;
        }

        reverse(res.begin(), res.end());

        return res;
    }
};

